      "text": "To find the midpoint of a segment, we use the midpoint formula. The midpoint \\( M \\) of a segment with endpoints \\( (x_1, y_1) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) \\) is given by:\n\\[ M = \\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right) \\]\n\nFirst, we need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) after translating segment \\( s_1 \\). Segment \\( s_1 \\) has endpoints \\( (4, 1) \\) and \\( (-8, 5) \\). Translating this segment by 2 units to the right and 3 units up involves adding 2 to the x-coordinates and 3 to the y-coordinates of each endpoint.\n\nFor the endpoint \\( (4, 1) \\):\n\\[ (4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4) \\]\n\nFor the endpoint \\( (-8, 5) \\):\n\\[ (-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8) \\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\( (6, 4) \\) and \\( (-6, 8) \\).\n\nNext, we use the midpoint formula to find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\). The x-coordinate of the midpoint is:\n\\[ \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2} = \\frac{0}{2} = 0 \\]\n\nThe y-coordinate of the midpoint is:\n\\[ \\frac{4 + 8}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6 \\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is:\n\\[ (0, 6) \\]\n\nPutting the final answer in the required format, we have:\n\\[ \\boxed{(0, 6)} \\]",
