      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the coordinates of the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) after it has been translated.\n\nThe original endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\).\n\nWhen segment \\( s_1 \\) is translated 2 units to the right and 3 units up, each endpoint of \\( s_1 \\) will move accordingly. We can find the new endpoints by adding 2 to the x-coordinate and adding 3 to the y-coordinate for each endpoint.\n\nFor the endpoint \\((4,1)\\):\n\\[\n(4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n\\]\n\nFor the endpoint \\((-8,5)\\):\n\\[\n(-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n\\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6, 4)\\) and \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\). The formula for the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of the endpoints of \\( s_2 \\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{6 - 6}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0,6)}\\).",
